# 01 — Hello LLM (TinyLlama chat)

```elixir
# Already installed in the firmware. Just verify we can see them.
Application.ensure_all_started(:arm_ai)
Application.ensure_all_started(:tokenizers)
```

## What this notebook does

Loads **TinyLlama 1.1B Chat** (a 1.1B-parameter transformer
quantised to 4 bits — about 700 MB on disk) and runs a single
generation from a prompt. The whole pipeline runs locally on
the phone's NEON-tuned CPU path, no network required.

Expected speed on FP3+ hardware: **~4–5 tokens/sec** for the warm
case, ~7–10 s first-call latency while the model loads.

## Load the model

```elixir
{:ok, model} = ArmAI.LlamaCandle.load("/data/models/tinyllama.gguf")
```

```elixir
{:ok, tokenizer} = Tokenizers.Tokenizer.from_file("/data/models/tinyllama-tokenizer.json")
```

## Compose a prompt

TinyLlama-Chat expects the ChatML template. Edit the `user`
variable below and re-evaluate.

```elixir
user = "What three things should I see in Brussels?"

system = "You are a helpful, concise travel guide."

prompt = """
<|system|>
#{system}</s>
<|user|>
#{user}</s>
<|assistant|>
"""

{:ok, encoded} = Tokenizers.Tokenizer.encode(tokenizer, prompt)
prompt_tokens = Tokenizers.Encoding.get_ids(encoded)
IO.puts("Prompt is #{length(prompt_tokens)} tokens.")
```

## Generate

`generate/2` runs under the performance CPU governor by default
(so the NEON kernels get the full clock budget for the burst, then
drop back to schedutil so the phone doesn't cook) — pass
`performance_governor: false` to opt out.

```elixir
{tokens, stats} =
  ArmAI.LlamaCandle.generate(model,
    prompt_tokens: prompt_tokens,
    max_new: 96
  )

{:ok, text} = Tokenizers.Tokenizer.decode(tokenizer, tokens)

IO.puts(text)
IO.puts("")
IO.puts("--- stats ---")
IO.inspect(stats)
```

## Try your own prompt

Change `user` above and re-evaluate the prompt + generate cells.
Subsequent runs reuse the already-loaded model and tokenizer,
so they're noticeably faster than the first one.

## What's happening under the hood

* `ArmAI.LlamaCandle` is the binary-API wrapper around
  [`candle-transformers`](https://github.com/huggingface/candle)
  (Rust, NEON-tuned matmul + dequant kernels).
* `Tokenizers` is the HuggingFace Rust tokenizer — fast and
  byte-accurate.
* `ArmAI.LlamaCandle.generate/2` scopes the CPU governor to
  `performance` on the big-cluster cores for the duration of the
  call, via `CpuGovernor.Performance.with_performance/1`.

If you want to swap models, drop another `.gguf` into
`/data/models/` and adjust the path. The TinyLlama family
(Phi-2 Q4, SmolLM-1.7B Q4, etc.) work with the same code.
